< The checkpoints are Phi-4-mini-reasoning (Phi-mini), Qwen3-4B, -8B and -14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-\allowbreak Llama-8B (DeepSeek-8B) and Granite-3.2-8B-\allowbreak Instruct (Granite-8B) with reasoning on \cite{microsoft2025phireasoning,yang2025qwen3,guo2025deepseek,ibmgranite2025}. For each budget $c$ in $\{0,\allowbreak 64,\allowbreak 128,\allowbreak 256,\allowbreak 512,\allowbreak 768\}$ we force exactly $c$ reasoning tokens by suppressing end-of-sequence, then append an answer prompt and take the most likely option letter; the free-text answer is also recorded. The prompt lists the options in randomized order, gives the objective as ``(private value $-$ b) $\times$ estimated win probability'' and asks the model not to infer the answer from option position. Greedy decoding runs under three option orders per scenario (648 decisions per model and budget); five sampled seeds run at 128, 384 and 768 tokens (seed s.d. of $q$ at most 0.017); a run without forcing lets each model stop within 1{,}024 tokens. A 768-token decision takes 9--35 seconds and its tokens cost $c_{768}=0.19$--$0.75$\textcent{} at RunPod rates \cite{runpod2026pricing}; break-even is $\text{B/e}=c_{768}/(q(768)-q(0))$, the best expected profit above which the expected gain $\max_b U(b)\,(q(768)-q(0))$ covers $c_{768}$. A synthetic version \cite{krishna2010auction} uses the same protocol on 216 scenarios with values 8--99 and integer bids, against two rivals with uniform values on 0--99 who bid two thirds of them, so payoffs are exact.
