> \paragraph{Letting the model finish.}
> With a 4{,}096-token cap, almost every trace of Qwen3-4B, -8B, -14B and DeepSeek-8B stops on its own (at most 1.1\% hit the cap), after a median of 980--1{,}390 tokens (Figure~\ref{fig:main}a). The Qwen models then pick the calculator's option in 99.7--100\% of decisions and recover 100\% [100, 100] of the gap; DeepSeek-8B recovers 99\% [98, 100], and 21 of its 25 wrong answers pass over a finished list. The tokens cost 0.45--1.11\textcent{} per decision, 1.5--2.1 times $c_{768}$, so break-even rises only to 2.0--4.8\textcent{}, against a best expected profit of at least \$1.75 in every scenario. For these models the fix is not to cap reasoning; the readers below matter where a cap is imposed for cost or latency.
